			<div class="content" id="post-content-4120588">
									

<p>I'm on the fence:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<ul>
<li>This isn't the <a href="/scp-2003">first</a> time <a href="/scp-1780">machine</a> I've seen the Foundation use &#8212; including a <a href="/scp-3176">personal favorite</a>. There's nothing novel about this variant.</li>
<li>There's numerous (minor, but still significant) errors in regards to punctuation (missing periods), capitalization, and grammar.</li>
<li>There's lots of parts where the article could be <em>significantly</em> trimmed without losing a thing.</li>
<li>There's a lot of moving parts here that I don't think are necessary (like the whole 'Protocol Nephilim').</li>
<li>I'm kind of 'meh' about the Silent Crusade's overwrought language (and even the description of their internal structure, which I think is probably not necessary here).</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li><strong>However:</strong> I <em>really</em> like the idea of the Foundation struggling with the moral weight of having a time-machine that works, and proof (via the Silent Crusade) that it's already been used to make the world a better place.</li>
<li>I also really like the mechanic of an addendum that's just a sheathe of papers that anyone could take off, and the proof that the author is not alone in their thinking caused by the sheathe still being there.</li>
<li>Plus the angle of Old Guard versus new Guard.</li>
<li>Finally, I like the implication (possibly unintentional, here) that the Silent Crusade might, in fact, be the Foundation defectors themselves &#8212; just displaced in time.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>All this adds up to a no-vote: I like the angle you're going for, but I think the execution could use significant tightening. I might come back to this later and give it a second read-through to see if I feel more strongly.</p>

