			<div class="content" id="post-content-4148823">
									

<p>Overall, this was kind of compelling to follow, but overlong and had fairly major issues.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Things I liked: Their various methods for changing history, the Foundation morality question, some of the aesthetics, the kind of feeling of investigating a mystery I got throughout reading it.</p>
<p>My issues were primarily with clinical tone (the point where I downvoted was when you said anywhen in the description) and, probably more importantly, with text economy and overwrought grammar. You spend so long getting everything across, and sometimes it feels like you're making sentences complex to sound clinical or cram in extra detail (including things that aren't super relevant). There's a few essays (<a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/clinical-vs-complex">here</a> and <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/essays-by-a-hack-writing-tales">here</a>) that might help with sentence structure, but paring back stuff is also important.</p>
<p>For actual content I had issues with, the tab about the ranks of crusaders felt generic and didn't add much. Them having a nearly 1 to 1 comparison to Foundation structure might have been foreshadowing, but it mostly felt like a lack of imagination. There were other sections that were similarly unhelpful or generic, such as the lecture about time and space. Repetition was also somewhat of an issue.</p>
<p>It's a little disappointing because that was an opportunity to give the GOI more character, which was otherwise lacking. For example, I get the impression that most of them wear union uniforms (which, by the way, need more context than &quot;US Union&quot; when first appearing) because their biggest operation was Gettysburg, so in all probability when you meet one of them, they were &quot;just there.&quot; But what kind of people are they? When are they from? (If they're from the time of the actual crusades, or, really, any time but now, hoo boy, you should be ready for non-21st-century morality.)</p>
<p>Also, like&#8230; you don't have to explain <em>everything</em> (e.g., all of their protocols, what all of the O5 notes actually mean), especially when a lot of your SCP is a single expository note. Give us some credit as readers, let us figure some things out. Similarly, if you had to explain iron sheaf in the additional stapled pages to set up the plot twist that also occurs in those pages, that's not really foreshadowing/a twist. Edit: Like I think someone said above: Nazis win WWII? Not really a twist that demands explanation.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

